export const coastalLessons=[
make('weathering','Weathering and mass movement',['1.3a'],[
 ['Break down, then move','Weathering breaks rock down in place. Mechanical weathering includes freeze–thaw: water enters cracks, freezes and expands; repeated cycles can widen the crack. Chemical weathering changes minerals through reactions. Biological weathering includes roots growing into cracks. These can interact.'],
 ['Two ways a slope can fail','In a slide, material moves along a broadly planar surface. In a rotational slump it turns along a curved slip surface, often leaving a stepped profile and tilted blocks. Gravity drives both. The model separates ideal cases; real landslides can combine movements.'],
 ['Why the cliff becomes less stable','Rain can saturate material, add weight and increase water pressure, reducing frictional resistance. Waves can remove support at the toe. Weathering can weaken rock. These changes can encourage failure; rainfall alone does not guarantee a slump, and a storm is not the only possible trigger.']
],[['Predict: roots widen a crack without carrying rock away. This is…',['Biological weathering','Longshore drift','Abrasion'],0],['Compare slide and slump. Which has a curved slip surface?',['Rotational slump','Planar slide','Both must be vertical falls'],0],['After rain, water pressure rises in a cliff. A defensible inference is…',['Slope failure may become more likely','Every cliff must collapse immediately','Gravity stops acting'],0],['Waves remove material from the cliff toe. This can…',['Reduce support for the slope','Turn all clay into granite','Prevent every mass movement'],0]],
[q('subaerial','p1','Water repeatedly freezes in a rock crack. Which process?',['Mechanical weathering','Sediment suspension','Biological growth'],0,'Freeze–thaw can break rock in place as ice expands.','weathering'),q('subaerial','p2','What drives a cliff slump directly?',['Gravity acting on material','The colour of the sea','Only sunlight'],0,'Gravity acts downslope; saturation or toe erosion can alter stability.','mass-movement'),q('subaerial','p3','A block rotates backwards as it moves on a curved surface. Identify it.',['Planar sliding','Rotational slumping','Dissolved transport'],1,'A curved slip surface produces rotation.','slumping')],
[q('subaerial','c1','Acidic water reacts with minerals in rock. This is…',['Chemical weathering','Traction','A planar slide'],0,'Chemical reactions alter minerals in place.','weathering'),q('subaerial','c2','Which observation best identifies a slide rather than a rotational slump?',['Movement along a relatively flat slip surface','Roots in a crack','A river in a valley'],0,'Sliding follows a broadly planar surface; slumping rotates along a curve.','mass-movement'),q('subaerial','c3','Why can saturation encourage slope failure?',['Water pressure can reduce resistance to movement','It always removes gravity','It instantly changes clay to basalt'],0,'Saturation may increase loading and pore-water pressure.','saturation'),q('subaerial','c4','Weathering and mass movement differ because…',['Weathering breaks down in place; mass movement moves material downslope','Both always dissolve rock','Neither affects a coast'],0,'Distinguish breakdown at a location from gravity-driven movement.','mass-movement')],'coast-weather'),
make('sediment','Erosion and sediment journeys',['1.3a'],[
 ['Four ways waves erode','Hydraulic action involves the force of water and pressure in cracks. Abrasion is sediment striking or scraping the coast. Attrition is particles colliding, becoming smaller and rounder. Solution dissolves susceptible material. Abrasion wears the coast; attrition wears the travelling particles.'],
 ['Follow the load','Large particles may roll along the bed: traction. Smaller grains can bounce: saltation. Fine particles can be carried within the water: suspension. Dissolved material moves in solution and is not visible as grains. Deposition happens when conditions no longer support transport of some of the load.'],
 ['A zigzag journey','Waves approaching at an angle drive swash obliquely up the beach; gravity draws backwash down its slope. Repetition can produce net longshore drift. Reverse the approach angle and drift can reverse. In our plan view the shoreline is horizontal and backwash runs straight down the beach; actual wave and current patterns are more complex.']
],[['Predict: pebbles scrape a cliff. Which erosion process?',['Abrasion','Attrition','Biological weathering'],0],['Compare the two drift directions. If wave approach reverses, net drift can…',['Reverse along the beach','Only remain to the right','Always move inland forever'],0],['Compare rolling, bouncing and suspended views. A bouncing sand grain shows…',['Saltation','Traction','Dissolved load'],0],['A current slows and can no longer carry some sand. What can follow?',['Deposition','Every grain dissolves immediately','All abrasion becomes root growth'],0]],
[q('sediment','p1','Two pebbles collide and become rounder. This is…',['Hydraulic action','Attrition','Chemical weathering'],1,'Attrition wears the sediment particles themselves.','erosion-types'),q('sediment','p2','A large pebble rolls along the bed. This is…',['Traction','Suspension','Solution'],0,'Traction moves bed load by rolling or sliding.','transport'),q('sediment','p3','What makes the backwash move down the beach slope?',['Gravity','Roots','The map key'],0,'Backwash returns downslope under gravity.','drift')],
[q('sediment','c1','Water pressure in a cliff crack is linked most directly to…',['Hydraulic action','Attrition','Forestry'],0,'Water impact and pressure can enlarge weaknesses.','erosion-types'),q('sediment','c2','Fine sediment carried within water is in…',['Suspension','Traction','A rotational slump'],0,'Suspended particles are carried within the fluid.','transport'),q('sediment','c3','Dissolved material travels…',['In solution, without visible solid grains','Only by bouncing','Only as boulders'],0,'Dissolved load is chemically in the water.','solution'),q('sediment','c4','Which combination supports longshore drift?',['Oblique swash and downslope backwash repeated','Swash always exactly perpendicular with no alongshore component','Only rock melting'],0,'Repeated zigzag motion can give net alongshore transport.','drift')],'coast-sediment'),
make('waves-geology','Waves meet the rocks',['1.3b','Integrated skill 5: introductory'],[
 ['Build up or draw back','Constructive waves typically have stronger swash than backwash, tending to build beach sediment. Destructive waves typically have stronger backwash than swash, tending to remove it. These are useful contrasting patterns, not a guarantee for every beach; sediment supply and conditions also matter.'],
 ['Read the rock bands','On a discordant coast, bands meet the coast roughly at right angles: less resistant rock can erode faster into bays, leaving resistant headlands. On a concordant coast, bands run broadly parallel. A resistant outer band can protect weaker rock until a breach lets erosion reach it.'],
 ['Weaknesses focus attack','Joints are fractures; faults are fractures with displacement. Water exploits weaknesses, while rock type, structure and wave exposure affect erosion. Our plan diagrams show relative resistance and band direction. They are not BGS maps; an authentic geology map and key are needed to investigate a named site.']
],[['Predict: swash is stronger than backwash. Which tendency?',['Beach building','Guaranteed rapid beach removal','Every cliff stops weathering'],0],['Compare band directions. Bands perpendicular to the shore are…',['Discordant','Concordant','Always identical rock'],0],['A resistant outer band is breached. Weaker rock behind it may…',['Erode more readily after access opens','Become immune to waves','Turn into a new glacier'],0],['A fault differs from a joint because a fault includes…',['Displacement along the fracture','Only a colour change','An absence of any fracture'],0]],
[q('waves','p1','Stronger backwash than swash is typical of…',['Destructive waves','Constructive waves','Only river floods'],0,'The returning water tends to carry beach sediment away.','wave-balance'),q('waves','p2','Rock bands parallel to a coastline describe…',['A concordant arrangement','A discordant arrangement','Proof of no erosion'],0,'Concordant refers to the broad structural arrangement, not an unchanging coast.','structure'),q('waves','p3','A headland survives longer than neighbouring weaker bands because…',['Its rock is relatively more resistant','Every headland is indestructible','All waves avoid every headland'],0,'Different resistance can cause differential erosion.','resistance')],
[q('waves','c1','A wave tends to build a beach when…',['Swash dominates backwash','Backwash dominates swash','Gravity has stopped'],0,'Constructive conditions favour net sediment addition, subject to supply.','wave-balance'),q('waves','c2','Alternating bands perpendicular to the coast can help produce…',['Headlands and bays','Only a straight immutable coast','Only new lava'],0,'Less resistant bands retreat faster than more resistant ones.','structure'),q('waves','c3','Which feature is a fracture without the displacement needed to define a fault?',['A joint','A beach','A saltating grain'],0,'Joints and faults both offer weaknesses, but faults have displacement.','weaknesses'),q('waves','c4','To connect a real coast’s rock units and form, use…',['A geology map with its key and coastal evidence','Only this invented coloured plan','Only today’s air temperature'],0,'Schematic relationships do not establish a named place’s mapped geology.','geology-evidence')],'coast-waves'),
make('weather-retreat','Weather, storms and retreat',['1.3c','Integrated skill 6: teaching data'],[
 ['Wind gives waves energy','Wave development depends on wind strength, how long it blows and fetch: the uninterrupted water distance over which it blows. Direction and coastal orientation affect exposure. Prevailing wind is a long-term frequent direction, not a prediction that every day has the same wind.'],
 ['Storms and seasons','UK winter conditions often bring more energetic storms, but damaging events can happen in other seasons. Large waves can erode the cliff toe; heavy rain may destabilise slopes. Storm surge raises sea level and can let waves reach further inland, especially with high tide. Wave attack, geology and slope condition interact.'],
 ['Calculate, then qualify','Invented teaching series: cumulative cliff retreat is 0m at year 0, 6m at year 2 and 15m at year 5. Mean over the whole period = (15−0)/(5−0) = 3m/year. Divide by elapsed years, not the number of observations. A mean does not show when retreat occurred or predict next year: one storm may cause a large jump.']
],[['Predict: same wind speed and duration, longer uninterrupted fetch. Wave growth has…',['More opportunity to gain energy','No possible relation to wind','A guaranteed identical height everywhere'],0],['Compare exposed and sheltered views. Wind direction matters because…',['Exposure depends on the direction waves approach the shore','All shores face the same way','Only rock colour controls waves'],0],['Teaching record: 0m at year 0 and 15m at year 5. Mean retreat?',['3m/year','5m/year','15m/year'],0],['A five-year mean is3m/year. Next year’s retreat…',['May differ; erosion is episodic','Must be exactly3m','Must be zero'],0]],
[q('weather','p1','A cliff retreats12m over 4 elapsed years. Mean rate?',['3m/year','8m/year','48m/year'],0,'12÷4=3m/year. The interval is four years.','mean-rate'),q('weather','p2','Prevailing wind means…',['A frequently occurring long-term wind direction','The wind must never change','A measure of cliff height'],0,'It describes a climatic pattern, not each day’s weather.','exposure'),q('weather','p3','Heavy rain can link to coastal retreat by…',['Encouraging saturation and slope instability','Removing all wave energy permanently','Making every rock equally resistant'],0,'Rainfall can alter slope stability while waves attack the toe.','storm-links')],
[q('weather','c1','Retreat increases from 4m to 22m between year 2 and year 8. Mean over this interval?',['3m/year','2.75m/year','18m/year'],0,'(22−4)÷(8−2)=18÷6=3m/year. Use changes in both variables.','mean-rate'),q('weather','c2','Which combination offers more potential for wave growth?',['Stronger sustained wind over a long fetch','No wind and no water','Only a change in cliff colour'],0,'Wind strength, duration and fetch influence wind-wave development.','wind-waves'),q('weather','c3','A storm surge combined with high tide can…',['Allow waves to reach higher parts of the coast','Guarantee that sea level falls everywhere','Stop every slope failure'],0,'Surge raises sea level; tide and waves influence the resulting impact.','storm-links'),q('weather','c4','A mean retreat rate alone cannot tell you…',['How retreat was distributed among individual storms and years','The arithmetic average over its stated interval','The rate’s units'],0,'An average can hide uneven events and is not an annual forecast.','mean-rate')],'coast-weather-data'),
make('erosional-landforms','How coastal landforms develop',['1.4a','Integrated skill 7: diagram introduction'],[
 ['Different retreat, different outline','Where alternating rock bands meet the sea, weaker bands may retreat into bays while resistant bands remain as headlands. Wave refraction can focus energy on exposed headlands. Cliff shape also reflects joints, faults, bedding, weathering and mass movement. Resistance alone does not explain every coastal form.'],
 ['Crack, cave, arch, stack','Hydraulic action and abrasion can enlarge weaknesses in a headland into caves. A cave may cut through to form an arch. Weathering and erosion weaken the roof; collapse can leave an isolated stack. This is one possible sequence with suitable structure and wave attack, not a compulsory lifecycle for every headland.'],
 ['Cliffs retreat, platforms remain','Wave attack around the foot can cut a notch. An unsupported overhang may collapse; removal of debris allows renewed attack and cliff retreat. Repeated retreat can leave a gently sloping wave-cut platform, often exposed at low tide. The model compresses time and idealises geometry; a real cliff may change by several interacting mechanisms.']
],[['Predict: weaker and resistant bands receive wave attack. The weaker band tends to form…',['A bay as it retreats more readily','An indestructible stack immediately','A new glacier'],0],['Compare cave, arch and stack. What separates the arch-to-stack stages?',['Collapse of the arch roof','Instant volcanic cooling','Sand dissolving into roots'],0],['A notch grows below a cliff overhang. This may…',['Reduce support and encourage collapse','Guarantee the cliff moves seaward','Stop gravity acting'],0],['A wave-cut platform is associated with…',['Repeated cliff retreat and erosion near its foot','Only a new motorway','Guaranteed deposition of a spit'],0]],
[q('forms','p1','Which process can enlarge a crack into a cave?',['Hydraulic action alongside abrasion','Only tree planting','Only the creation of a map legend'],0,'Water pressure and sediment impact can exploit weaknesses.','cave-sequence'),q('forms','p2','An isolated rock pillar left after arch-roof collapse is a…',['Stack','Bay','Swash'],0,'The remaining detached pillar is a stack.','cave-sequence'),q('forms','p3','Why can a wave-cut platform become visible at low tide?',['Falling water exposes the eroded rock surface','The rock instantly rises with every tide','All platforms are beaches of loose sand'],0,'The platform may lie in the zone uncovered as the tide falls.','cliff-retreat')],
[q('forms','c1','Less resistant rock between resistant bands often erodes into…',['A bay','A guaranteed arch','A new granite intrusion'],0,'Differential erosion can leave a bay between headlands.','headlands-bays'),q('forms','c2','Which sequence is physically plausible under suitable conditions?',['Cave enlarges → arch forms → roof collapses → stack remains','Stack melts → rainforest forms → arch freezes','Every cave instantly becomes a beach'],0,'Link each stage to erosion and eventual collapse, not a fixed timetable.','cave-sequence'),q('forms','c3','Repeated notching, collapse and removal can lead to…',['Cliff retreat leaving a wave-cut platform','Guaranteed permanent advance of every cliff','A coast unaffected by waves'],0,'Continued attack and collapse can move the cliff line inland.','cliff-retreat'),q('forms','c4','Why should the model’s stages not be treated as a forecast?',['Structure, exposure and interacting processes vary between real coasts','Every headland has the same shape and timing','A model always predicts exact years'],0,'It explains a conditional causal sequence, not a named site’s future.','model-limits')],'coast-landforms')
];
